> is not x even?
  note: "not" of a number: read as bitwise not, ~, not logical not, ! (say "bitwise not" or "logical not")
  note: "even": read as an even function, f(-x) = f(x), not an even number for every input (say "an odd function" or "always odd")
  ucas: it(x) = ~x
  ucas: parity it
  note: x has no type: inferred int from the integer operations in the body
parity of it:
  neither even nor odd
  not even: f(x) = -2, f(-x) = 0 at x = 1
  not odd: f(x) = -2, f(-x) = 0 at x = 1
  proved, exhaustive over every x with -x in the domain
> is not not x even?
  note: "even": read as an even function, f(-x) = f(x), not an even number for every input (say "an odd function" or "always odd")
  ucas: it(x) = ~(~x)
  ucas: parity it
  note: x has no type: inferred int from the integer operations in the body
parity of it:
  odd: f(-x) = -f(x)
  not even: f(x) = 1, f(-x) = -1 at x = 1
  proved, exhaustive over every x with -x in the domain
> is not not not x even?
  note: "even": read as an even function, f(-x) = f(x), not an even number for every input (say "an odd function" or "always odd")
  ucas: it(x) = ~(~(~x))
  ucas: parity it
  note: x has no type: inferred int from the integer operations in the body
parity of it:
  neither even nor odd
  not even: f(x) = -2, f(-x) = 0 at x = 1
  not odd: f(x) = -2, f(-x) = 0 at x = 1
  proved, exhaustive over every x with -x in the domain
